ing with  voters online and in many ways this is  changing not just the face of campaign  organizing but also of the data that  is being collected well campaigns had  classically engaged with voters in a way  that was fundamentally built around the  voter file around defining a list of  voters to start with calling those  people directly tracking those  one-on-one engagements there are new  challenges specifically when we know  that that social media is the first  place that many voters are ever going to  interact with a campaign that will have  interaction points there that aren't  directly mapped back to individual  behaviors on individual behaviors we can  track on a voter file and it raises both  new opportunities but also new  challenges in our ability to track the  effectiveness of programs when we've  traditionally been able to measure  whether a program works based on an  individual record of the voter file  which ultimately comes back with a  record of whether or not that person  turned out in the election that we were  running program around when we start to  think about measuring online engagement  in a really serious way we need to start  moving beyond that voter file contained  view of people to understand more  holistically how people are interacting  in the world whether or not we're able  to map them directly back to a human on  the voter file and that foundation of  democratic data this starts with  understanding how are we as campaigns  engaging with voters online how are  people engaging with our web sites and  social media activities what things are  they responding positively or negatively  to but fundamentally a truly 360 degree  view of voters requires not just  understanding our own interactions with  campaigns on with voters online but also  understanding how voters are being  affected by discussion by other targeted  messaging that happens on the Internet  one starting point of that is that we're  working within the Democratic Party to  bui